Notebook-AI @VERSION@  -  Linux (@ARCH@) distribution
=====================================================

Offline handwritten-text OCR for the desktop: open photos or scans, prepare them (rotate / straighten / crop),
extract the text, review and correct it, keep many pages in one project file, export UTF-8 text.
Nothing leaves your machine: there is no network access at run time.

WHAT THIS PACKAGE CONTAINS
  app/           the application (JavaFX 21 and ONNX Runtime for Linux x86-64 only) and the OCR models
                 (PP-OCRv6_small detector + recognizer, Apache-2.0) - everything needed to run OCR offline
  install.sh     per-user installer          uninstall.sh   removes what install.sh installed
  run-notebook-ai.sh   run in place, without installing
  scripts/ licenses/ LICENSE THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt   helper + legal documents
  distribution.json  SHA256SUMS            metadata and checksums of every shipped file

WHAT YOU MUST INSTALL YOURSELF (not included)
  * Java 21 or newer (a JRE is enough). No Java runtime is bundled. Check with:  java -version
      Debian/Ubuntu/Kali:  sudo apt-get install openjdk-21-jre
  * A graphical desktop session (X11 or Wayland/XWayland) to open the window.
  * Standard Unix tools used by the start scripts (sh, sed, uname, xargs): present on any normal Linux system.
  Supported: Linux x86-64 only. Other systems/architectures (ARM, macOS, Windows) need their own distribution.

STEP 1 - EXTRACT
  Extract the archive anywhere you like (spaces in the path are fine). If your extraction tool dropped the
  executable bits, restore them:      chmod +x install.sh uninstall.sh run-notebook-ai.sh app/bin/notebook-ai
  Optional integrity check of the archive itself:   sha256sum -c notebook-ai-@VERSION@-linux-@ARCH@.zip.sha256

STEP 2a - JUST RUN IT (no installation)
  ./run-notebook-ai.sh
  It works from any current directory, checks your Java version, and writes nothing into this folder.
  Headless self-test (no window):   ./run-notebook-ai.sh --self-check

STEP 2b - INSTALL FOR YOUR USER (no sudo needed)
  ./install.sh
  Installs to   ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/notebook-ai   with the command   $HOME/.local/bin/notebook-ai
  The installer verifies SHA256SUMS first, prints where everything went, and tells you if ~/.local/bin is not
  on your PATH (it never edits your shell startup files). Then run:   notebook-ai
  The installed command keeps working if you move or delete this extracted folder.
  Other locations:   ./install.sh --app-dir DIR --bin-dir DIR      ./install.sh --help
  System-wide copy (needs sudo, not used by default):   sudo ./install.sh --prefix /usr/local

UPGRADE
  Extract the new archive and run its ./install.sh again. The old installation is replaced as a whole (no stale
  files are mixed in) and restored automatically if anything fails. Your projects (.nbai) are never touched.

UNINSTALL
  ./uninstall.sh            (same --app-dir/--bin-dir/--prefix options as you installed with)
  Removes only the installation owned by the installer. Your projects, exports and other programs stay.
  If you deleted this folder: remove  ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/notebook-ai  and
  $HOME/.local/bin/notebook-ai  yourself, or extract the same version again and run ./uninstall.sh.

IF SOMETHING GOES WRONG
  "Java 21 or newer is required"  -> install Java 21+ or point JAVA_HOME at it.
  "failed its SHA256SUMS integrity check" -> the extraction is damaged; extract the archive again.
  "notebook-ai: command not found" after installing -> add the printed directory to your PATH, open a new terminal.
  An older notebook-ai earlier on PATH -> the installer names it and prints the full path of the new one.

LICENSES
  Notebook-AI is BSD 3-Clause (LICENSE). Third-party components and their licenses are listed in
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt; the texts are in licenses/. Please read the "known gaps" section there.

